.TH "vsearch-cigar" "5" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
cigar \[em] a compact text-based format for representing pairwise
alignments
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
CIGAR (Compact Idiosyncratic Gapped Alignment Report) is a text-based
format that encodes a pairwise alignment as a sequence of run-length
encoded operations.
Each operation describes a column of the alignment, or a run of
identical consecutive columns, as seen from the query viewpoint.
Hence, a cigar string encodes query modifications needed to equal the
target.
However, beware that cigar strings in the SAM format adopt the target
viewpoint: they encode target modifications needed to equal the query
(see \f[V]vsearch-sam(5)\f[R]).
vsearch emits CIGAR strings in several search, clustering, and pairwise
alignment outputs (see SEE ALSO).
.PP
A CIGAR string is an ASCII string consisting of one or more
\f[I]operations\f[R].
Each operation is written as an optional non-negative integer
\f[I]run-length\f[R] immediately followed by a single uppercase letter
indicating the \f[I]operation type\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
[run-length] operation [run-length] operation ...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For example, \f[V]3M2I3MD\f[R] describes an alignment that starts with
three matches or mismatches, followed by two insertions (residues
present only in the target), three matches or mismatches, and a single
deletion (a residue present only in the query).
.SS Operations
.PP
vsearch recognizes three operation types, corresponding to the three
possible column types in a gapped pairwise alignment (see
\f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R]):
.TP
\f[V]M\f[R]
Match or mismatch.
The column aligns one query residue with one target residue.
vsearch does not distinguish matches from mismatches in its CIGAR
strings.
.TP
\f[V]I\f[R]
Insertion.
A residue is present in the target but absent from the query (a gap in
the query); it must be inserted into the query to equal the target.
.TP
\f[V]D\f[R]
Deletion.
A residue is present in the query but absent from the target (a gap in
the target); it must be deleted from the query to equal the target.
.PP
Other operation letters defined by the SAM specification (\f[V]X\f[R],
\f[V]=\f[R], \f[V]N\f[R], \f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]H\f[R], \f[V]P\f[R]) are
never produced by vsearch.
.SS Run-length encoding
.PP
Consecutive columns of the same operation type are grouped into a single
run.
The number of columns in a run is written as a decimal integer
immediately before the operation letter, without leading zeros.
A run-length of 1 is implicit and omitted: vsearch writes \f[V]M\f[R],
not \f[V]1M\f[R], and \f[V]MID\f[R] rather than \f[V]1M1I1D\f[R].
Run-lengths are positive and bounded by the alignment length; there is
no maximum total length for a CIGAR string.
.SS Empty and missing alignments
.PP
An empty CIGAR string is valid and represents an empty alignment.
.PP
When a command reports that a query has no alignment to a target, the
CIGAR field is replaced with a placeholder whose value depends on the
output format:
.IP \[bu] 2
the \f[V]aln\f[R] and \f[V]caln\f[R] userfields (\f[V]--userout\f[R])
produce an empty field;
.IP \[bu] 2
the \f[V]--samout\f[R] (SAM) and \f[V]--uc\f[R] (UCLUST-like) outputs
produce the single character \f[V]*\f[R].
.PP
(The \f[V]--blast6out\f[R] tabular output contains no CIGAR field at
all.)
.SS Exact-match shorthand
.PP
As a vsearch-specific convention, a CIGAR string consisting of the
single character \f[V]=\f[R] is emitted in the \f[V]--uc\f[R] output
(and only there) when the query and the target sequences are strictly
identical; with \f[V]--cluster_fast\f[R], terminal gaps are ignored when
deciding identity.
This shorthand is not part of the SAM specification; \f[V]--samout\f[R]
and the \f[V]caln\f[R] userfield always emit explicit operations.
.SS Ill-formed strings
.PP
vsearch never reads CIGAR strings from its input files: the strings it
writes are always well-formed as described above, and there is no
user-facing path that parses one back.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
The following alignment of an 8-nt query against a 9-nt target
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
query:     ACGT--TACG
target:    AC-TGGTTCG
alignment: MMDMIIMMMM
cigar:     2M1D1M2I4M
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
contains, from left to right:
.IP \[bu] 2
2 columns with matching residues (\f[V]AC\f[R] / \f[V]AC\f[R]),
.IP \[bu] 2
1 deletion (\f[V]G\f[R] present only in the query, a gap in the target),
.IP \[bu] 2
1 column with matching residues (\f[V]T\f[R] / \f[V]T\f[R]),
.IP \[bu] 2
2 insertions (\f[V]GG\f[R] present only in the target, gaps in the
query),
.IP \[bu] 2
4 columns with matching residues (\f[V]TACG\f[R] / \f[V]TTCG\f[R]).
.PP
Without run-length encoding, the alignment reads \f[V]MMDMIIMMMM\f[R],
after encoding it reads \f[V]2M1D1M2I4M\f[R].
Note that \f[V]M\f[R] does not distinguish matches from mismatches; a
mismatching column is encoded with the same operation letter.
